WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.330
The sad fact of life is that even superheroes
die.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.710
Of course, unlike the rest of us, they always
come back to life.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.969
But while a lot of superheroes have pretty
straightforward deaths, Tony Stark's trips

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:11.300
to the hereafter are downright weird.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.040
1987's "Armor Wars" told the story of Tony
Stark finding out that villains were using

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.600
his designs to power their own armored costumes
and then doing everything he could to take

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.600
that technology back.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.740
It also featured the first time that Iron
Man "died."

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.280
Sort of.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.340
The thing is, the armored Avenger didn't go
down swinging while battling to save the world,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.660
he mainly died because it was the best PR
move.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.960
While it might seem like a good idea to take
his inventions back from the bad guys, Tony's

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.630
quest also meant taking on S.H.I.E.L.D. and
beating up Stingray, a third-string Avenger.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.329
Even worse, Tony then took a trip to Russia,
where he caused an international incident

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:48.289
and accidentally killed an old rival, the
Titanium Man.

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:51.900
With a growing list of international crimes
stacking up, Tony realized that there was

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.460
only one thing left to do in order to keep
both his personal reputation and his company's

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.860
from being completely destroyed.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.700
He made a full confession and explained he
was doing it for the greater good.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.859
"You want my property - you can't have it!

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.800
But I did you a big favor.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.960
I have successfully privatized world peace."

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.020
Nah, just kidding!

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.250
Instead he created a false identity named
"Randall Pierce," claimed that Pierce been

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.659
the one wearing the armor all this time, then
faked Randall's death.

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:22.100
The next month, there was a brand new Iron
Man in brand new armor who just happened to

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.620
sound like, act like, and know all the same
things as the dearly departed Mr. Pierce.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.340
Faking your death is like eating a potato
chip.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.030
You can't stop at just one.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.700
This time, it started when Tony was shot by
an ex-girlfriend in 1989, which left him unable

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.240
to walk without his armor.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.310
Since using technologies to deal with injuries
had worked so well before, Tony decided to

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.159
implant some microchips in his back, and accidentally
created other physical problems that started

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.040
to kill him anyhow.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:47.600
Oops.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.700
After Tony spent some time heroically threatening
to murder Justin Hammer unless he sold him

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.180
his company for one dollar, Stark contacted
his lawyer, made his final arrangements, and

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.140
apparently died.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:02.080
In reality, he went into cryogenic stasis
until another scientist could sort out his

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.720
spinal software problems, all while letting
even close friends like Rhodey think he was

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:06.280
dead.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.960
This one gets complicated.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.000
Back in 1995, Marvel published The Crossing,
a storyline that revealed that Tony Stark

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.830
had secretly been murdering people behind
the scenes for years, including killing a

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.530
fellow Avenger called Yellowjacket on the
doorstep of Avengers Mansion.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.350
It wasn't entirely his fault, though.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.980
It turned out that Tony had been manipulated
by a time-traveling supervillain named Immortus.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.000
When it was all revealed, Tony completely
lost it, and wound up fighting the Avengers

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.640
and a younger version of himself before finally
sacrificing his life to stop the plot he'd

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.080
been an unwilling part of.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.020
With that, Tony Stark was dead, going out
in one of the most ignoble deaths a superhero

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:43.720
has ever experienced.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.620
The good news, though, is that all of this
happened when, and because, very few people

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.660
were actually reading Iron Man comics, so
nobody really cared.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:52.900
So about that younger version.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.240
When Tony Stark was revealed to have gone
bad like a carton of year-old milk, the Avengers

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.750
had to take action and decided that what they
really needed to do was go to another dimension

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.450
and get a teenage version of Tony, and then
make him fight his own murderous adult self.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.490
Really, guys?

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.450
You couldn't have sent Thor and the Hulk to
take care of him instead?

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.160
"Uh, what?"

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.200
It might surprise you to find out that this
plan did not work out very well!

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.060
Fortunately, after the old Tony died, the
new version survived… for about ten months.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:24.080
Then came Onslaught, a crossover in which
a mingling of minds between Professor X and

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.790
Magneto produced an all-powerful third entity.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.670
Onslaught initially appeared as a hulking,
armored figure that looked like someone had

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:35.290
tried to make Magneto entirely out of knives,
but when the heroes teamed up to stop him,

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.720
they discovered that this wasn't even his
final form.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:42.760
Instead, he evolved into a being of pure intangible
psychic energy, and unless they could contain

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.590
Onslaught in some kind of physical body, they'd
never be able to destroy him.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.820
The catch was that Onslaught fed on "mutant
energy," so if any of the X-Men tried to finish

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.480
what their boss started, Onslaught would only
get stronger.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.520
Thanks to that little catch, the Avengers
and Fantastic Four jumped into Onslaught's

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.460
brain-tornado and broke him to manageable
bite-size chunks that all the mutants could

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.660
zap into dust.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.370
With that, Teen Tony had heroically sacrificed
himself to save the world… only to be reborn

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:12.040
as an adult Tony on an imaginary Earth created
by Reed Richards' son, and then reborn once

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.840
again back on regular Marvel Earth a year
later.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:18.260
In short, 1996 was a hell of a year for Iron
Man fans.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.450
Once Tony had returned to Earth and adulthood,
he created a new suit that he nicknamed the

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:24.450
"Safe Armor."

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.940
True to its name, it was a little bulkier
and more defensive than some of the other

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.560
suits, and even had an advanced artificial
intelligence that was designed to keep its

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.090
occupant from being harmed.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.530
Needless to say, that advanced AI did what
every advanced AI in comics does: it became

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:42.070
sentient and immediately went evil, something
that no one could've predicted when Tony used

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.600
pieces of Ultron in order to create it.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:45.690
You know, Ultron?

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.750
The genocidal robot that once massacred an
entire country?

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.500
Yeah, nobody could have possibly seen this
coming.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.680
"You're unbearably naive."

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.140
The story played out with the armor acting
like an obsessive ex-lover, a bit of subtext

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.790
that was only made less subtle once the armor
went full Fifty Shades and tied Tony up while

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.340
shouting things like "Get inside me, Tony."

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:09.160
The whole time, however, it was creating an
advanced nervous system that would allow it

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:13.120
to feel the full range of human emotion, and
while it had apparently saved things like

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.260
"not killing people" for last, they showed
up just in time.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:20.270
When Tony had a heart attack while they were
fighting, the armor tore its own "heart" out

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.020
and used it to save his life.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.380
After that, Tony buried the armor, complete
with a grave marker that read "Here lies Iron

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:27.240
Man: Avenger."

